This `spawn` on `t` hands its task the destination `out`, which a task of `r` also reaches: the scopes one `using` statement opens join one after the other natively, the last declared first, while under Node each task runs where it is spawned, so the tasks of sibling scopes share no lock, no atomic and no destination; open each scope in a block of its own, so the first joins before the second opens, `{ using r = scope(); r.spawn(f, 1, out, 0) } { using t = scope(); t.spawn(f, 3, out, 2) }`
